<p>After setup the site is technically running, but empty. This order has proven useful:</p>

<h2>1. Choose a theme</h2>
<p><strong>Administration → Themes</strong> chooses the look. The default theme is there from the start; more come from the Packages page, for example Indigo (a calm, light marketplace theme) or a trade-specific one such as Audigo for auctions. The logo, icon and description are under <strong>Themes → Branding</strong>.</p>

<h2>2. Choose an extension</h2>
<p><strong>Administration → Packages</strong> installs an extension from its repository or from an uploaded zip file, <strong>Administration → Extensions</strong> switches it on. Only one extension is active at a time: enabling one switches the previously active one off, its data stays. Without an active extension the site runs as a plain account-and-pages system, with no catalogue.</p>

<h2>3. Basic settings</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Administration → Settings</strong>: the site's name, the sender address for mail, whether registration is open, the default description, the currency, whether providers and offers are checked before publication, the languages offered and the default one.</p>

<h2>4. Payment methods, if orders are sold</h2>
<p>With a catalogue extension running, <strong>Administration → Payment methods</strong> switches on the ways to pay for orders. Each provider sets up their own payment account; the platform never holds money. See the chapter "Payment methods for orders".</p>

<h2>5. Subscriptions, if the operator sells plans</h2>
<p>This is independent of the marketplace: the operator sells plans directly, with invoices. See the chapter "Subscriptions" for the setup step by step.</p>

<h2>6. Legal pages</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Administration → Pages</strong>, create the imprint, terms, privacy policy, and, where needed, the withdrawal and report forms. A legal role exists only once.</p>
